EXACTLY HOW REGISTER BITS ARE REARRANGED.

Experimental English Major C architectural source specification.
Target: ARMv7-A in T32 state, unprivileged execution.
Status: eight independently named operations, not an exhaustive encoding
inventory, nor an executed language implementation.

The long operation names that actually grant permission are operations
65 through 72 in the sibling document:
"All and only these initial operations are permitted.english".
This document spells out what those names mean, which information they
consume, what state they change, and what they deliberately do not do.

NORMATIVE ARCHITECTURAL DEPENDENCY.

ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R edition,
ARM DDI 0406C.d (ID040418), especially the A6 Thumb decode
tables and the relevant A8 instruction entries.

SECONDARY CROSS-CHECK USED FOR THIS SLICE.

ARM Compiler armasm Reference Guide, ARM DUI 0802B, ID121814,
2014, Chapter 3:
  Section 3.25, "BFC and BFI", printed pages 3-47 and 3-48;
  Section 3.70, "REV, REV16, REVSH, and RBIT", pages 3-125 and 3-126;
  Section 3.72, "SBFX and UBFX", page 3-129.
The secondary manual covers architectures beyond the pinned ARMv7-A
profile; it does not automatically authorize such extensions.
Public copy:
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f3fa899428f7a6b3328fd44

An independent diagnostic assembly was also decoded by clang/LLVM
version 17, targeting armv7a-none-eabi with T32 selected, solely to
obtain concrete instruction halfwords. That diagnostic reference is
recorded in the associated example file. It is NOT an ICK compiler
result or a qualified project build.

PART ONE. WHAT THE MACHINE CAN AND CANNOT INFER FROM THIRTY-TWO BITS.

A 32-bit register contains bits numbered 0 through 31.
Bit zero is the least significant bit. Bit 31 is the most significant.
An eight-bit portion with low bit number 8 contains bits 8..15.
A sixteen-bit portion with low bit number 16 contains bits 16..31.

Do not confuse:
  the numerical significance order of bits in a register;
  the byte order of multibyte objects when stored in memory;
  the ordering of instruction halfwords in a T32 instruction stream;
  the way a virtual address is mapped to physical DRAM;
  the length of a native instruction encoding.

The operations in this file work on register bit patterns.
They perform NO memory loads and NO memory stores. The memory
endianness of an external object is not changed by any one of them.
They do not modify the CPSR endian-state bit. They do not rewrite
an instruction sequence, grant the right to execute memory, or
modify the execution index except through normal sequencing.

A result may be mathematically interpreted as unsigned or signed.
Only operations 67 and 72 explicitly make a sign-extension choice.
The others produce their specified 32-bit pattern without
asserting the programmer's eventual interpretation of those bits.

PART TWO. DIFFERENT KINDS OF REVERSAL.

OPERATION 65, reverse the FOUR BYTE POSITIONS of a register value.

Given a 32-bit value with component bytes, from most significant
to least significant, A:B:C:D, return D:C:B:A. In bit-position terms:
  result bits 31..24 receive original bits 7..0;
  result bits 23..16 receive original bits 15..8;
  result bits 15..8 receive original bits 23..16;
  result bits 7..0 receive original bits 31..24.
No other bits may influence the result.
A source of 0x12345678 yields 0x78563412.

The selected native form is the TWO-byte Thumb T1 REV, not
the separate four-byte encoding nor a sequence of shifts.
Its encoding uses a low-register source and low-register destination,
each chosen from R0-R7. Preserve all condition flags.

OPERATION 66, reverse bytes independently WITHIN EACH HALFWORD.

Given a value A:B:C:D, return B:A:D:C.
Never cross the sixteen-bit dividing line between source bits
15 and 16. The first output halfword comes exclusively from
the original first halfword. The second output halfword comes
exclusively from the original second halfword.
A source of 0x12345678 yields 0x34127856.

The selected native form is TWO-byte T1 REV16 with low
registers R0-R7. It does not set flags or load memory.

OPERATION 67, reverse only the LOW HALFWORD and sign-extend.

Let source bits 15..8 be A and source bits 7..0 be B.
Form the sixteen-bit value B:A, discarding the original
source bits 16..31 entirely. If bit 15 of B:A is zero,
produce 0x0000 followed by B:A; if it is one,
produce 0xFFFF followed by B:A. This conditional filling
does not make an ARM conditional branch and does not
modify the comparison flags.

For source low halfword 0x80FF, B:A becomes 0xFF80.
Its sign bit is one, so the resulting 32-bit bit pattern
is 0xFFFFFF80, representing -128 if later read as a
signed 32-bit two's-complement integer.

The selected native form is two-byte T1 REVSH.
The word "signed" in the English name is part of
the actual operation and cannot be silently dropped.

OPERATION 68, reverse EVERY INDIVIDUAL BIT.

For every source bit position k, 0<=k<=31,
result bit position 31-k receives original bit k.
This is not REV, which preserves the ordering of
bits WITHIN each individual byte.

The 32-bit pattern 0x0000000D has its least significant
four bits 1101 in conventional most-significant-first
binary notation. Bit reversal gives 0xB0000000.
Another example: 0x00000001 becomes 0x80000000.
Applying bit reversal twice to the same 32-bit value
recovers the original value.

The selected native form is FOUR-byte T32 RBIT and
requires general registers R0-R12 (not SP or PC).
There is no two-byte RBIT form in this selected T32 ISA.

PART THREE. ZEROING A CONTIGUOUS INTERVAL.

OPERATION 69, clear a chosen bitfield.

The input consists of:
  an old destination value D, held in a general register;
  a first selected bit position called first_bit;
  a strictly positive number of selected bits called width.

The last selected position is first_bit+width-1.
The operation is defined only for:
  0 <= first_bit <= 31;
  1 <= width <= 32-first_bit.
For all positions k inside the selected interval, set the
destination bit to ZERO. For all other positions, keep
the exact bit from the original destination D.

Example:
  original D     = 0xA5A5FFFF;
  first_bit      = 8;
  width          = 8;
  allowed mask   = 0x0000FF00;
  resulting D    = 0xA5A500FF.
The source is a single old register; there is no other
register whose bits must be incorporated in the result.

The selected machine instruction is BFC, FOUR bytes in
the Thumb T1 encoding. Its bits describe the highest
cleared position and the starting position. An
out-of-range width or highest bit must be rejected.
The instruction does NOT preserve the cleared bits
in some invisible temporary register.

PART FOUR. REPLACING ONE BIT INTERVAL WITH ANOTHER REGISTER'S BITS.

OPERATION 70, insert a chosen bitfield.

There are TWO register inputs: the old destination D
and the source S. Their bit patterns are read as
the old architectural state before any destination write.

The selected destination interval is
[first_bit, first_bit+width-1] inclusive.
Bits outside the interval are copied from old D.
Within the interval, result bit at first_bit+j
receives source bit j for j from zero to width-1.

For old D=0x12345678, old S=0x000000AB, first_bit=8,
width=8, the resulting register contains 0x1234AB78.

No source bits above bit width-1 are incorporated.
The operation does not copy the full source register,
and does not move any destination bits left or right.

If the source register name equals the destination
register name, the old bit pattern is used for both
operand roles. An implementation must not overwrite
D before it has computed the specified result.
A single permitted machine instruction can realize
such an aliasing case where the exact encoding allows
it, but legality still belongs to the pinned ISA entry.

The selected encoding is FOUR-byte BFI, T1 Thumb.
The allocated interval is an actual encoded field,
not an unbounded shift count, and no flags change.

PART FIVE. TWO DIFFERENT MEANINGS OF FIELD EXTRACTION.

OPERATION 71, extract an UNSIGNED field.

Starting from source bit first_bit, copy width successive
source bits into the lowest width bits of the destination,
maintaining their original relative bit order.
Every destination bit from width up through bit 31
must be zero.

For source S=0x000005A0 and first_bit=4,
width=7, the selected bits yield 0x5A.
The destination must become 0x0000005A,
irrespective of its previous value.

This is UBFX, FOUR-byte T32 encoding T1. The source
register is read, and the destination register is
overwritten. The architecture's width-minus-one
encoding must not be mistaken for a width of one less
than the requested semantic width.

OPERATION 72, extract a SIGNED field.

Perform the same interval selection and position
translation as operation 71, but interpret the
highest selected bit as the sign of a width-bit
two's-complement integer. Copy that highest
selected bit to ALL higher destination positions.

For source S=0x000000E0 and first_bit=2,
width=6, the six-bit extracted pattern is 111000.
Its top selected bit is one. As a signed six-bit
two's-complement quantity, it represents -8.
The 32-bit destination bit pattern is 0xFFFFFFF8.
It is not 0x00000038, which would be the unsigned
interpretation of the same selected interval.

When the highest selected bit is zero, sign extension
fills higher bits with zero and can coincide with
unsigned extraction. That coincidence does not
make the operation names interchangeable.

This is SBFX, FOUR-byte T32 T1. Source and destination
registers are restricted to R0-R12.

PART SIX. CONDITIONAL EXECUTION IS A SEPARATE EFFECT.

The eight bit-rearrangement operations do not set
N, Z, C or V. If a subsequent instruction uses
a conditional branch depending on Z or V, the
flags observed by that branch are preserved through
one of these operations.

When a particular permitted machine encoding legally
appears inside a T32 IT block, the existing ITSTATE
determines whether its data-register effects occur.
The bit operation does not independently create,
enter or exit an IT block. The interpreter still
advances across the whole two-byte or four-byte
encoded instruction when its condition is false,
and advances ITSTATE according to architectural rules.

A proposed lowering may not change a flag-preserving
operation into a flag-setting arithmetic construction
and call the two sequences equivalent merely because
the destination values agree. The later branch might
observe different flags.

PART SEVEN. REASONS TO KEEP THE ALTERNATIVES.

Choice A: use the dedicated REV, REV16, REVSH and RBIT
encodings. Benefit: each operation's data permutation
is explicit and can be matched to one native instruction.
Cost: a different target ISA may not have equivalent
single instructions, and the source cannot assume
that architectural compactness proves execution speed.

Choice B: implement reversal with ordinary shifts,
logical operations and masks. Benefit: more portable
to machines lacking a particular dedicated operation.
Cost: multiple intermediate steps and possible effects
on flags and registers. Each instruction in an actual
resulting disassembly must be translated independently;
the result alone does not justify inventing a single RBIT.

Choice C: perform byte swapping during memory load
or store using an explicitly chosen representation.
Benefit: may avoid a separate register permutation.
Cost: changes the memory-access and representation
contract; unaligned accesses, permissions, hardware
endianness and other observers become important.
A REV instruction does not, by itself, do that.

Choice D: implement a bitfield insertion or clear by
AND, OR and shifts. Benefit: same mathematical value
may be obtained using familiar bitwise operations.
Cost: may change comparison flags depending on exact
encoding, and incorrect masks can change unrelated bits.

Choice E: use BFC/BFI/SBFX/UBFX when those instructions
are available and all bit indices are encodable.
Benefit: one exactly named bitfield operation.
Cost: width/position limits and register restrictions
must remain visible in source; these operations
do not handle arbitrary-length bit strings.

Choice F: perform signed versus unsigned extraction
by explicit shifts. Benefit: makes sign propagation
observable and can be expressed on many machines.
Cost: arithmetic-right versus logical-right shift,
intermediate width, and top selected sign bit require
an exact proof. Replacing SBFX with UBFX based on a
few nonnegative fixtures would be a correctness defect.

Choice G: classify these patterns as instruction
decoding work rather than ordinary numerical work.
Benefit: building an interpreter may want to extract
bitfields from raw instruction halfwords as DATA.
Restriction: successfully extracting a field from
bytes does not turn a malformed encoding into a
permitted executable operation. Decode legality,
ITSTATE, privileges and executable permissions
remain separate.

The language currently grants the dedicated operations.
No universal performance, compilation strategy, or
target-independent preference follows from that choice.

PART EIGHT. REJECTED SHORTCUTS.

A valid transformation MUST NOT:
  * describe bytes as memory-ordered when they are
    only numbered bitfields inside a register;
  * call REV and RBIT the same operation;
  * call REVSH a zero-extending operation;
  * alter N/Z/C/V while claiming to use the
    flag-preserving variants named here;
  * use SP or PC as a data register for any of these forms;
  * represent an invalid zero-width or over-running
    bitfield as a successfully executed native instruction;
  * omit the old destination as an input to BFI;
  * substitute bitfield extraction for signed extension
    without proving the selected sign bit;
  * turn 32-bit T32 instructions into two consecutive
    16-bit instructions in an English control-flow trace;
  * manufacture a code-execution permission by writing
    or rearranging bits that resemble an instruction;
  * claim that all possible aliases and encodings
    of these assembler mnemonics were admitted.

PART NINE. WHAT HAS ACTUALLY BEEN VERIFIED.

Source files and independently inspected diagnostic
assembler halfwords establish a concrete correlation
between representative encodings and English bit
effects. The source rules and numerical examples
have NOT been accepted by an English Major C runtime,
AICI, ARM hardware, or a full formal verification.

The complete ARM DDI 0406C.d table still contains
numerous ungranted instruction classes and encoding
variants. This document leaves that uncompleted
work explicit.
